\begin{abstract}
The rapid development of artificial intelligence has positioned large language models (LLMs) as fundamental components of intelligent legal systems. However, these models face significant limitations in legal dispute analysis, including insufficient depth of legal knowledge representation, limited understanding of legal concepts, and inherent reasoning deficiencies. To address these challenges, this research proposes an enhanced framework for legal dispute analysis that integrates prompt engineering with a multidimensional knowledge graph architecture. The framework introduces a three-stage hierarchical prompt structure comprising task definition, knowledge background, and reasoning guidance components, supplemented by legal-specific reasoning path templates and a dynamic prompt optimization mechanism to establish a comprehensive legal reasoning guidance system. Concurrently, a three-layer knowledge graph architecture is constructed, consisting of legal classification ontology, legal representation, and legal instance layers. Four complementary methods facilitate precise dynamic retrieval of legal concepts: direct matching of legal norm codes, legal domain-specific semantic vector similarity measurement for establishing semantic associations, path reasoning based on legal ontology relationship graphs for concept logic connections, and specialized lexical segmentation matching mechanisms for term-level identification. These components are subsequently integrated with web search technology to establish a knowledge-enhanced ecological framework supporting robust legal decision-making processes. Experimental results demonstrate that this integrated framework significantly improves model performance in legal dispute analysis, promoting more accurate legal application analysis when handling complex legal cases while exhibiting a nuanced understanding of judicial decision-making logic, thus providing a novel technical approach for the practical implementation of intelligent legal assistance systems.
\end{abstract}

