\section{Background}\label{sec:bg}
% maekawa, BLLB, 3-colorings, what is a Miura-ori
An \textit{origami crease pattern} is a straight line drawing of a planar graph $C=(V,E)$ on a region $A \subseteq\R^2$. The \textit{Miura-ori} \cite{originalMiura} is a crease pattern formed by crossing parallel lines with zig-zags, creating faces that are all congruent parallelograms (see Figure \ref{fig:miura}). We denote an $m$ by $n$ Miura-ori by $M_{m,n}$ and denote the face in the $i$th row, $j$th column as $\alpha_{i,j}$. The case $M_{2,n}$ will have a single row of vertices, which we call $x_1,...,x_{n-1}$ from left to right. For $k=1,\ldots, n-1$, we label the crease left of $x_k$ as $e_{3k-3}$, the crease above as $e_{3k-1}$, the crease to the right as $e_{3k}$, and the crease below as $e_{3k+1}$, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:edgelabel}. We additionally set $e_1=e_0$ to describe the leftmost crease. 

