\subsection{Vertices of degree 2}\label{subsec:deg2}
% diameter proof, using 3-colorings
One peculiar discovery we made was that despite the number of vertices and edges in $\OFG(M_{2,n})$ increasing exponentially, the number of vertices of degree $2$ always remained constant no matter how large $n$ was. We show that on the $\OFG(M_{2,n})$, there exist 4 vertices of degree 2 for all $n \ge 2$. To prove this, we first prove two lemmas about the flippability of corner faces of $M_{2,n}$.

\begin{lemma}[Common-Column-Corners Lemma]\label{lem:CCC}
    For all $n \ge 2$, two corner faces in the same column (the faces $\alpha_{1,1}$ and $\alpha_{2,1}$ or the faces $\alpha_{1,n}$ and $\alpha_{2,n}$) cannot both be unflippable for a valid MV assignment $\mu$ on $M_{2,n}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Any corner face is incident to exactly one vertex of $M_{2,n}$. Assuming that we have a valid MV assignment $\mu$, we use the rules for determining if a face of $M_{2,n}$ is flippable in Section \ref{sec:bg} and notice that the creases around a single vertex can only ever make diagonal faces unflippable (i.e. $\alpha_{1,1}$ and $\alpha_{2,2}$, or $\alpha_{1,2}$ and $\alpha_{2,1}$). Thus, corner faces in the same column cannot both be unflippable.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
    For all $n \in \N$, there are no vertices of degree $0$ or $1$ in $\OFG(M_{2,n})$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Common-Row-Corners Lemma] \label{lem:CRC}
    For all $n \ge 2$,  two corner faces in the same row (the faces $\alpha_{2,1}$ and $\alpha_{2,n}$ or the faces $\alpha_{1,1}$ and $\alpha_{1,n}$) cannot be the only flippable faces for a valid MV assignment $\mu$ on $M_{2,n}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    For the sake of contradiction, suppose we have a valid MV assignment $\mu$ on $M_{2,n}$ where there are exactly $2$ flippable faces, $\alpha_{2,1}$ and $\alpha_{2,n}$. If $n=2$, then we use the same rules for flippability from Section \ref{sec:bg} as we did in the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:CCC} since the creases around a single vertex can only ever make diagonal faces unflippable. Assume $n > 2$. We split the proof into two cases: one where $x_1$ and $x_{n-1}$ have the same majority MV-parity, and one where $x_1$ and $x_{n-1}$ have opposite majority MV-parity. We argue that each case gives us a contradiction. Without loss of generality, assume $x_1$ is a majority mountain vertex.\\

    \textit{Case 1.} Both $x_1$ and $x_{n-1}$ are majority mountain.

    As shown in Figure \ref{fig:CRCcaseone}, to make $\alpha_{2,1}$ flippable, it must be bordered by $2$ mountain creases. Assigning the crease above $x_1$ to be a valley and the crease to the right of $x_1$ to be a mountain makes $\alpha_{1,1}$ and $\alpha_{2,2}$ unflippable. To make the last face incident to this vertex, $\alpha_{1,2}$, unflippable, we must look to $x_2$ and again assign the crease above it to be a valley and the rest mountains, again making the top-left and bottom-right faces relative to $x_2$ (i.e. $\alpha_{1,2}$ and $\alpha_{2,3}$) unflippable. We continue this pattern for $x_3$, $x_4$, and so on as desired to make other faces unflippable from the left. However, if we continue this pattern for all vertices, then at $x_{n-1}$ we would make the bottom-right face unflippable, which is $\alpha_{2,n}$, which would give us a contradiction. We attempt to avoid this by creating a different pattern from the rightmost vertex.\\

    To make $\alpha_{2,n}$ flippable, we assign the crease below $x_{n-1}$ to be a valley crease and the rest are assigned to be mountain creases. This makes the top-right and bottom-left faces (i.e. $\alpha_{1,n}$ and $\alpha_{2, n-1}$) unflippable. Extending this pattern to $x_{n-2}$, $x_{n-3}$, and so on makes other faces unflippable from the right, but again extending this pattern for all vertices would make $\alpha_{2,1}$ unflippable. So, the only option remaining is to use both patterns simultaneously and have them meet somewhere in the middle.\\

    With these MV-patterns around $x_1$ and $x_{n-1}$, we extend both of them simultaneously to try to make all faces other than $\alpha_{2,1}$ and $\alpha_{2,n}$ unflippable. If the left, red pattern in Figure $\ref{fig:CRCcaseone}$ terminates at some vertex $x_k$ and the blue pattern at $x_{k+1}$, both patterns assign the crease $e_{3k}$ that is incident to both $x_k$ and $x_{k+1}$ to be a mountain. At this point, we have assigned all creases in $M_{2,n}$ an MV-parity, but the face $\alpha_{1,k+1}$, which is bordered by red and blue creases, must be flippable, giving us a contradiction.\\

