    Together, cases $1$ and $2$ show that $\alpha_{2,1}$ and $\alpha_{2,n}$ cannot be the only flippable faces for a valid MV assignment of $M_{2,n}$. The case of $\alpha_{1,1}$ and $\alpha_{1,n}$ follows the same logic by symmetry, completing the proof.
\end{proof}

Using these two lemmas, we prove that there are always exactly $4$ vertices of degree $2$ on $\OFG(M_{2,n})$ by showing that the MV assignments these vertices correspond to are when the only $2$ flippable faces are in opposite corners.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:oppositeexist}
    For all $n \ge 2$, there are exactly $4$ vertices of degree $2$ on $\OFG(M_{2,n})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    Suppose there are $2$ flippable faces on a valid MV assignment $\mu$ on $M_{2,n}$. By the Common-Column-Corners Lemma, one of these faces is in column $1$ and one is in column $n$. By the Common-Row-Corners Lemma, these faces must be in different rows. This creates $2$ cases where exactly $2$ faces are flippable: one where $\alpha_{1,1}$ and $\alpha_{2,n}$ are flippable, and one where $\alpha_{2,1}$ and $\alpha_{1,n}$ are flippable (i.e. the only flippable faces are in opposite corners). In each case, we get $2$ valid MV assignments to choose from, giving us a total of $4$ valid MV assignments with $2$ flippable faces, which are explicitly constructed in Figure \ref{fig:deg2construction}.
\end{proof}

