We first calculate $h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)$. We construct our path in a boustrophedon pattern in the same way as the construction of the bijection between Miura-ori MV assignments and 3-colored grid graphs (see Figure \ref{fig:edgeweights}).  
By construction and our choice of $\gamma$, 
$$\begin{matrix}\begin{cases}
w(\gamma,\overrightarrow{v_{1,j}v_{1,j+1}})= 1, & 1\leq j<n\\
w(\gamma,\overrightarrow{v_{1,n}v_{2,n}})=-1\\
w(\gamma,\overrightarrow{v_{2,j}v_{2,j-1}})=-1, & 1<j\leq n
\end{cases} &\text{    } &\begin{cases}
w(\beta,\overrightarrow{v_{1,j}v_{1,j+1}})= -1, & 1\leq j<n\\
w(\beta,\overrightarrow{v_{1,n}v_{2,n}})=1\\
w(\beta,\overrightarrow{v_{2,j}v_{2,j-1}})=1, & 1<j\leq n
\end{cases}\end{matrix}$$
Thus, we have 
$$h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v_{i,j})=\begin{cases}
-2(j-1), & i=1, 1\leq j\leq n\\
-2(n-1)+2(n-(j-1)), & i=2, 1\leq j\leq n
\end{cases}$$
We then calculate the minimum distance $\ell$ by dividing into three cases: $H\leq -2, -2<H\leq 2n-2, H>2n-2$. 

\textit{Case 1}. $H\leq -2$:
\begin{align*}
    \ell&\geq \frac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})} |H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)|
    =\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n |H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v_{i,j})|\\
    &=\frac{1}{2} \left(\sum_{j=1}^n 2(j-1)-H\right) + \frac{1}{2} \left(\sum_{j=1}^n 2(n-1)-2(n-(j-1))-H\right)\\
    &=n^2-2n-Hn\geq n^2
\end{align*}

\textit{Case 2}. $H> 2n-2$: 
\begin{align*}
    \ell&\geq \frac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})} |H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)|\\
    &=\frac{1}{2} \left(\sum_{j=1}^n -2(j-1)+H\right) + \left(\frac{1}{2} \sum_{j=1}^n -2(n-1)+2(n-(j-1))+H\right)\\
    &=-n^2+2n+Hn>n^2
\end{align*}

\textit{Case 3}. $0<H\leq 2n-2$: 
\begin{align*}
    \ell \geq {} & \frac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})} |H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)|
    =\frac{1}{2} \left(\sum_{j=1}^n |-2(j-1)+H|\right)\\
    & + \frac{1}{2} \left(\sum_{j=1}^n |-2(n-1)+2(n-(j-1))+H|\right)
    = \frac{1}{2} \left(\sum_{j=1}^{\frac{H}{2}+1} -2(j-1)+H \right) \\
    & + \frac{1}{2} \left(\sum_{j=\frac{H}{2}+2}^n 2(j-1)-H \right) 
    +\frac{1}{2} \left(\sum_{j=\frac{H}{2}+2}^n 2(n-1)-2(n-(j-1))-H\right)\\
    & +\frac{1}{2} \left(\sum_{j=1}^{\frac{H}{2}+1} -2(n-1)+2(n-(j-1))+H\right)
    ={}  n^2-Hn+\frac{H^2}{2}+2H-2n+2.
\end{align*} 


This reaches a minimum at $H=n-2$ with $\ell\geq \frac{n^2}{2}$ when $n$ is even and $H=n-1$ or $n-3$ with $\ell\geq \frac{n^2+1}{2}$ when $n$ is odd (since $H$ has to be even). Thus, accounting for whether $n$ is even or odd gives us $\ell\geq \lceil \frac{n^2}{2} \rceil$, which is the lowest bound of the three cases. By Lemma~\ref{lem:MiuraOFGconn} we know that the Miura-ori flip graph is connected, so by Lemma \ref{lem:3colshortestpath} there exists a shortest path in $R_3(M_{2,n}^*) \cong \OFG(M_{2,n})$ between opposite degree $2$ vertices that is exactly length $\lceil\frac{n}{2}\rceil$. 
\end{proof}

Therefore, the shortest distance between the opposite degree-2 vertices in $\OFG(M_{2,n})$ is $\lceil \frac{n^2}{2}\rceil$.  We will now show that the shortest distance between any two vertices is never longer than this distance. %To do this we will appeal to some results on reconfiguration graphs and the Miura-ori origami flip graph from the literature.






Referring back to Lemma \ref{lem:3colshortestpath}, we want to know at which $H=H_{v_{1,1}}^R(\beta)$ value $\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})} |H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)|$ is minimized, where $\gamma$ and $\beta$ are corresponding 3-colorings of two arbitrary MV assignments on $M_{2,n}$, as that would determine the shortest path length between any two MV assignments. Let $y_{i,j}=H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v_{i,j})$. Let $Y=\{y_{i,j}\mid 1\leq i\leq 2, 1\leq j\leq n\}$ be the multiset of all $y_{i,j}$-values. We list the elements of $Y$ from smallest to largest, and define the \textit{median values of $Y$} to be the two ``middle" elements of $Y$ when written from lowest to highest, which we label $y_{a,b},y_{c,d}$. (Note that the multiset $Y$ has an even number of elements.)

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:2diff}
    The $y_{i,j}$ values in $Y$, sorted from lowest to highest, can only differ from one value to the next by 0 or 2.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Since $H=H_{v{1,1}}^R(\beta)$ is fixed, we need to show that $h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)$ changes by 0, $-2$, or 2 as we move from a vertex $v$ in $M_{2,n}$ to one of its neighbors. This is a simple consequence of the definition $h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)=w(\beta,P_{v_{1,1}, v})-w(\gamma,P_{v_{1,1}, v})$. Since our 3-colorings are proper, as we move from $v$ to one of its neighbors, $w(\beta,P_{v_{1,1}, v})$ and $w(\gamma,P_{v_{1,1}, v})$ will both change by $\pm 1$. If both change by the same amount, $h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)$ will not change. If they change by different amounts, $h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)$ will change by 2.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:median}
    The sum $\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})} |H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)|=\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n |y_{i,j}|$ is minimized when at least one of the median values of $Y$ is $0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Suppose $0$ is not one of the median values of $Y$. Then, either both median values are positive or negative (they can never be one positive and one negative as this would skip 0 in $Y$ and violate Lemma~\ref{lem:2diff}). Without loss of generality, suppose both median values are positive. Then, there exists at least $n+1$ positive values in $Y$. 

    We show that the sum $\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}|$ is not minimal. Let $y_{a,b},y_{c,d}$ be the two positive median values, with $y_{a,b}\leq y_{c,d}$. Consider $H'= H-y_{a,b}$ and $y_{i,j}'=H'+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v_{i,j})$. %Note that $H'$ is a valid value since $H$ and $y_{a,b}$ are both even integers, so $H'$ is even as well. Recall that we can choose any even integer for $H$ since it corresponds to how many times we flip $v_{1,1}$ to get a specific color permutation. Since we don’t care about color permutations in MV assignments, we can just choose the color permutation, and thus the $H$ value, that minimizes the sum. \lumi{wasn't this said earlier?} 
    For all the positive $y_{i,j}\in Y$, we have 
$$|y_{i,j}'|=-y_{a,b}+|y_{i,j}|.$$
Thus, all the positive values ``decrease” by $y_{a,b}$. For the negative values, 
$$|y_{i,j}'|=y_{a,b}+|y_{i,j}|,$$
so all the negative values ``increase” by $y_{a,b}$ .Since we have at least $n+1$ positive values in $Y$ and at most $n-1$ negative values of $Y$, the sum $\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}|$ decreases by at least $2y_{a,b}$ to $\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}'|$. In other words, 
$$\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}'|\leq -2y_{a,b}+\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}|.$$
Since we can find a different $H'$ such that $\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}'|<\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}|$, $\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})} |H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)|$ is not minimized, which is a contradiction. Thus, one of the median values of $Y$ must be $0$ for the sum $\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}|$ to be minimized. %\lumi{I feel like this is missing a sentence to the effect of ``this is a contradiction, implying \{insert statement\}}
\end{proof}


\begin{theorem}\label{thm:shortestpath}
    The two vertices with a maximal shortest path between them are two opposite vertices of degree $2$. In other words, if we have any two MV assignments $\mu_1,\mu_2$, then there exist corresponding 3-colorings $\gamma,\beta$ such that %\lumi{I think this should be ``there exist corresponding 3-colorings $\gamma,\beta$ such that,"} Then,  
$$\frac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})}|H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)|\leq \left\lceil \frac{n^2}{2}\right\rceil,$$
where $H$ is chosen such that $\frac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})}|H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)|$ is minimized. %\lumi{restating it in different terms is good! me likey}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    Let $\tilde{\gamma},\tilde{\beta}$ be the 3-colorings of $M_{2,n}^*$ corresponding to the MV assignments where only $\alpha_{1,n}$ and $\alpha_{2,1}$ are flippable. We know $\frac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})}|H+h_{\tilde{\gamma},v_{1,1}}(\tilde{\beta},v)|=\lceil \frac{n^2}{2}\rceil$. To show this is the diameter, we want to show $\frac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})}|H+h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v)|\leq\frac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})}|H+h_{\tilde{\gamma},v_{1,1}}(\tilde{\beta},v)|$, for any two 3-colorings $\gamma,\beta$ that correspond to MV assignments $\mu_1,\mu_2$.

    Let $\tilde{y}_{i,j}=H+h_{\tilde{\gamma},v_{1,1}}(\tilde{\beta},v_{i,j})$. We first calculate $\tilde{Y}=\{\tilde{y}_{i,j}\mid 1\leq i\leq 2, 1\leq j\leq n\}$ such that $\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|\tilde{y}_{i,j}|=\sum_{v\in V(M^*_{2,n})}|H+h_{\tilde{\gamma},v_{1,1}}(\tilde{\beta},v_{i,j})|$ is minimal. We calculate $h_{\tilde{\gamma},v_{1,1}}(\tilde{\beta},v_{i,j})$ as in Theorem \ref{thm:minopposite2} to get 
$$\tilde{h}_{i,j}=h_{\tilde{\gamma},v_{1,1}}(\tilde{\beta},v_{i,j})=\begin{cases}
-2(j-1), & i=1, 1\leq j\leq n\\
-2(n-1)+2(n-(j-1)), & i=2, 1\leq j\leq n
\end{cases}$$
In the case where $n$ is even, we get $\{\tilde{h}_{i,j}\mid 1\leq i\leq 2, 1\leq j\leq n\}=\{-2n+2,-2n+4,-2n+4...,0,0,2\}$. The median values of this multiset  
are both $-n+2$ so setting $H=n-2$ gets us $\tilde{y}_{i,j}=H+\tilde{h}_{i,j}$ and $\tilde{Y}=\{-n,-n+2,-n+2,...,0,0,...,n-2,n-2,n\}$. Taking the absolute value of the elements in this multiset gets 
$$\{|\tilde{y}_{i,j}|\mid 1\leq i\leq 2n-1\} = \{0,0,2,2,2,2,...,n-2,n-2,n-2,n-2,n,n\}.$$
This multiset contains two $0$'s, two $n$'s (which correspond to the maximum and absolute value of the minimum $\tilde{y}_{i,j}$ values), and four of every even integer in between. 

In the case where $n$ is odd, we still get $\{\tilde{h}_{i,j}\mid 1\leq i\leq 2, 1\leq j\leq n\}=\{-2n+2,-2n+4,-2n+4,...,0,0,2\}$ but the median values are now $-n+3$ and $-n+1$ Setting $H=n-3$ gets us $\tilde{Y}=\{-n-1,-n+1,-n+1,...,0,0,...,n-3,n-3,n-1\}$. Taking the absolute value of the elements in this multiset gives us 
$$\{|\tilde{y}_{i,j}|\mid 1\leq i\leq 2, 1\leq j\leq n\} = \{0,0,2,2,2,2,..., n-1,n-1,n-1,n+1\}.$$
This multiset contains two $0$’s, three $n-1$’s (one of which corresponds to the maximum $\tilde{y}_{i,j}$ value), one $n+1$ (which corresponds to the absolute value of the minimum $\tilde{y}_{i,j}$ value) and four of every even integer in between. Note that setting $H=n-1$ gets us the exact same $\{|\tilde{y}_{i,j}|\mid 1\leq i\leq 2, 1\leq j\leq n\}$, except that $n+1$ would instead correspond to the maximum value of $\tilde{Y}$ and one of the $n-1$ values would instead correspond to the absolute value of the minimum value of $\tilde{Y}$.

We now show that $\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}|\leq \sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|\tilde{y}_{i,j}|$, where $y_{i,j}\in Y$ is created from any two three colorings $\gamma,\beta$ such that $\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^n|y_{i,j}|$ is minimal.  

We claim that $Y$ has at least 2 of every element that is not the maximum or minimum. To show this is true, consider the directed cycle $K^*$ on $M_{2,n}^*$
consisting of edges $\overrightarrow{v_{1,1}v_{1,2}},\overrightarrow{v_{1,2}v_{1,3}},...,\overrightarrow{v_{1,n}v_{2,n}},\overrightarrow{v_{2,n}v_{2,n-1}},...,\allowbreak\overrightarrow{v_{2,2}v_{2,1}},\overrightarrow{v_{2,1}v_{1,1}}$. %In the 3-coloring $\tilde{\gamma}$, the weight of the edges $\overrightarrow{v_{1,1}v_{1,2}},..., \allowbreak\overrightarrow{v_{1,n-1}v_{1,n}}$ and $\overrightarrow{v_{2,1}v_{1,1}}=-\overrightarrow{v_{1,1}v_{2,1}}$ are all $1$ and the weight of the edges $\overrightarrow{v_{1,n}v_{2,n}},\overrightarrow{v_{2,n}v_{2,n-1}}...,\allowbreak\overrightarrow{v_{2,2}v_{2,1}}$ are all $-1$. Thus, adding up the weight of all the edges gets us a weight of $0$ for $K^*$.
Assign to each vertex of $M_{2,n}^*$ its respective $h_{i,j}=h_{\gamma,v_{1,1}}(\beta,v_{i,j})$ value. Let the vertex with the maximum $h_{i,j}$ value be $v_M$ and the minimum be $v_m$. Since $K^*$ is a cycle, there are two paths of $h_{i,j}$ values that can be taken from the minimum to the maximum along the cycle, and we know $h_{\gamma, v_m}(\beta,v_M)$ is the same in either case by Lemma \ref{lem:weightindependence} (See Figure \ref{fig:hicycle}). We know from one vertex to the next along either path, the $h_{i,j}$ value either increases or decreases by $2$ or stays the same (by Lemma~\ref{lem:2diff}). Thus, no matter which path we take, the $h_{i,j}$ values of the vertices along both paths are all even integers between the minimum and maximum. Thus, all the $H+h_{i,j}$ values in between the minimum and maximum have to appear at least twice in $Y$. This means that when we find the $H$ such that $Y$ is minimal, the multiset $\{|y_{i,j}|\mid 1\leq i\leq 2, 1\leq j\leq n\}$ contains at least two $0's$ and at least four of every even integer in between $0$ and the maximum or the absolute value of the minimum (depending on which one is smaller). 

