\title{Bayesian Discrete Diffusion Beats Autoregressive Perplexity}
\author[1]{Cooper Doyle}
\date{July 05, 2025}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We reveal a hidden Bayesian core of discrete diffusion language models by showing that the expected denoiser output over the forward corruption distribution recovers the exact posterior over clean tokens.  Under minimal assumptions, Monte Carlo marginalization converges to this posterior at rate $O(1/\sqrt{K})$, providing a simple proof of consistency and finite‐sample error bounds.  Exploiting this insight, we implement a lightweight inference‐time ensemble that averages $K$ corrupted predictions to yield “posterior‐aware” generation and uncertainty estimates for free.  Our method achieves a perplexity of 8.8 with $K=8$ on WikiText-2, compared to 20.3 for GPT-2, demonstrating that Bayesian discrete diffusion can substantially outperform a similar sized autoregressive baseline.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}

Modern large language models (LLMs) have achieved impressive fluency and coherence, yet their overconfidence and lack of reliable uncertainty estimates pose serious risks in safety‐critical applications such as healthcare, law, and autonomous systems.  While autoregressive transformers produce high‐quality text, they rarely provide trustworthy measures of epistemic uncertainty without costly ensembling or auxiliary calibration techniques \cite{guo2017calibration,hendrycks2017baseline}.  

Recently, Jingyang Ou et al.\ introduced RADD (\emph{Reparameterized Absorbing Discrete Diffusion}) \cite{ou2025absorbing}, showing that the discrete diffusion “concrete score” can be expressed in closed‐form as a conditional probability of the clean token and that caching these scores yields accelerated sampling and state‐of‐the‐art perplexities among diffusion‐based LMs.  However, RADD has not been framed as exact Bayesian posterior inference, nor has its implicit uncertainty signal been fully exploited.

In this work, we reveal and leverage the \emph{hidden Bayesian core} of RADD (and any discrete denoiser trained on an absorbing mask‐and‐denoise objective).  Concretely, we show:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Exact Posterior Equivalence.}  \emph{Proposition~1} proves that the expectation of the denoiser under the forward mask distribution recovers the true Bayesian posterior \(p(x_0\!\mid x)\).  \emph{Theorem~2} then establishes that Monte Carlo marginalization converges at rate \(O(1/\sqrt K)\) with finite‐sample Hoeffding bounds, and we identify the “Jensen slack” that explains why the \(K=1\) point sits off the \(1/\sqrt K\) line.
  \item \textbf{Perplexity Breakthrough.}  A lightweight inference‐time ensemble of \(K=8\) mask‐and‐denoise passes reduces perplexity on WikiText-2 from 20.3 (GPT-2 small) down to 8.8—without any additional training or parameter overhead.
  \item \textbf{Uncertainty “For Free.”}  From the same marginal posterior we obtain principled uncertainty diagnostics (predictive entropy, marginal variance, error‐vs‐entropy curves) with no extra compute beyond the \(K\) forward passes.
\end{enumerate}

Together, these contributions recast discrete diffusion language models as exact Bayesian inference engines that outperform much larger autoregressive baselines in both accuracy and uncertainty quantification, all at a modest constant‐factor cost under inference.  

\section{Background \& Related Work}

\paragraph{Discrete Diffusion and RADD.}

Discrete diffusion models extend continuous‐diffusion’s mask-and-denoise paradigm to text by randomly replacing tokens with a special \textsc{[MASK]} symbol and training a transformer to reconstruct the original sequence. While early work on continuous diffusion showed that the denoiser approximates the Bayesian posterior \(p(x_0\!\mid x_t)\) and that marginalization yields exact inference \cite{ho2020denoising,song2021score}, discrete variants have largely been treated as heuristic predictors. Ou \emph{et al.} (2024) first observed that, in the absorbing-mask setting of RADD, one can derive a closed-form “concrete score” equal (up to a known scalar) to the true conditional \(p(x_0\!\mid\tilde x)\) \cite{ou2025absorbing}. In this work, we go further: we prove that the \emph{expected} output of \emph{any} discrete denoiser under its masking schedule \emph{exactly} equals the full posterior \(p(x_0\!\mid x)\), and we provide finite-sample convergence and error bounds.


\paragraph{Monte Carlo Marginalization \& Ensembles.}
Monte Carlo sampling has long been used to approximate intractable posteriors: bagging \cite{breiman1996bagging}, dropout as Bayesian inference \cite{gal2016dropout}, and deep ensembles \cite{lakshminarayanan2017deep} all rely on averaging multiple stochastic forward passes.  While related ideas have been applied in supervised and continuous‐diffusion settings, to our knowledge no one has explicitly framed discrete‐diffusion denoisers as Bayesian models and then deployed MC marginalization at inference time to improve perplexity and quantify uncertainty.

\paragraph{Autoregressive Scaling vs.\ Inference-Time Ensembles.}
Large autoregressive LLMs (e.g.\ GPT-2/3) achieve strong perplexity by scaling up parameters, but they provide limited uncertainty “for free” and often demand calibration or external ensembling to become reliable \cite{guo2017calibration,hendrycks2017baseline}.  Our work shows that, for a much smaller discrete‐diffusion model, a simple inference-time ensemble recovers the exact Bayesian posterior—dramatically lowering perplexity and yielding well‐behaved predictive entropy without any additional training or model scaling. 

\section{Bayesian Posterior via Corruption Marginalization}

\subsection{Notation and Setup}
Let \(x = (x_{1},\dots,x_{L})\in\mathcal{V}^L\) be a sequence of discrete tokens from a vocabulary \(\mathcal{V}\) of size \(V\).  At time \(t\in[0,1]\), the forward “absorbing” corruption is
\[
q_t(\tilde x \mid x)
\;=\;\prod_{i=1}^L \bigl[(1-\beta_t)\,\delta_{\tilde x_i,\,x_i} + \beta_t\,\delta_{\tilde x_i,\,\textsc{[MASK]}}\bigr],
\]
where \(\delta\) is the Kronecker delta, \(\textsc{[MASK]}\) is the absorbing token, and \(\beta_0=0\), \(\beta_1=1\).

A learned denoiser \(P_\phi(x\mid\tilde x)\) is trained to approximate the true reverse posterior \(p(x\mid\tilde x)\).  We now show that if \(P_\phi\) were exact, then marginalizing over the forward noise recovers the exact Bayesian posterior.

\subsection{Proposition}
\begin{proposition}[Posterior Equivalence]
Suppose the denoiser \(P^\star\) satisfies
\[
P^\star(x_0 \mid \tilde x) \;=\; p(x_0 \mid \tilde x)
\quad\text{for all clean sequences }x_0\text{ and corruptions }\tilde x.
\]
Then
\[
\mathbb{E}_{\tilde x \sim q_t(\cdot\mid x_0)}
\bigl[P^\star(x_0 \mid \tilde x)\bigr]
\;=\;
\sum_{\tilde x} q_t(\tilde x \mid x_0)\,p(x_0\mid \tilde x)
\;=\;
p(x_0\mid x_0)\;=\;1.
\]
More usefully, for any candidate sequence \(x\),
\[
\mathbb{E}_{\tilde x \sim q_t(\cdot\mid x_0)}
\bigl[P^\star(x \mid \tilde x)\bigr]
\;=\;
\sum_{\tilde x} q_t(\tilde x \mid x_0)\,p(x\mid \tilde x)
\;=\;
p(x\mid x_0).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By the law of total probability,
\[
p(x\mid x)
=\sum_{\tilde x}p(x,\tilde x\mid x)
=\sum_{\tilde x}q_t(\tilde x\mid x)\,p(x\mid \tilde x).
\]
Since \(P^\star(\cdot\mid\tilde x)=p(\cdot\mid\tilde x)\), its expectation under \(q_t\) coincides with the right‐hand side.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Theorem (Consistency \& Finite‐Sample Error)}
\begin{theorem}
Let \(\{\tilde x^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^K\) be i.i.d.\ draws from \(q_t(\cdot\mid x)\), and define
\[
\hat p^{(K)}(x)
\;=\;\frac1K\sum_{k=1}^K P_\phi(x\mid \tilde x^{(k)}).
\]
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \(\hat p^{(K)}(x)\xrightarrow{\mathrm{a.s.}}p(x\mid x)\) as \(K\to\infty\).
  \item For any \(\epsilon>0\),
  \[
  \Pr\bigl(\|\hat p^{(K)} - p\|_\infty > \epsilon\bigr)
  \;\le\;2\,V\;\exp\bigl(-2K\epsilon^2\bigr),
  \]
  since each coordinate of \(P_\phi(\cdot\mid\tilde x)\) lies in \([0,1]\).
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch]
For any fixed token \(v\in\mathcal{V}\), the random variable \(Z_k = P_\phi(v\mid\tilde x^{(k)})\) is bounded in \([0,1]\).  By the Strong Law of Large Numbers,
\[
\frac1K\sum_{k=1}^K Z_k
\;\xrightarrow{\mathrm{a.s.}}\;\mathbb{E}[Z_k]
\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{\tilde x\sim q_t}[P_\phi(v\mid\tilde x)]
\;=\;p(v\mid x).
\]
Hoeffding’s inequality then gives \(\Pr(|\hat p^{(K)}(v)-p(v\mid x)|>\epsilon)\le2\exp(-2K\epsilon^2)\), and a union bound over all \(V\) vocabulary entries yields the sup‐norm bound.
\end{proof}

\section{Practical Marginalization Inference}

Building on our theoretical posterior‐equivalence, we now present a clean, self‐contained inference recipe and show how all of our downstream metrics (generation, uncertainty, calibration) fall out of it “for free.”

\subsection{Algorithm Overview}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Monte Carlo Marginalization for Discrete Diffusion}
\label{alg:marginalize}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require clean input sequence \(x\in\mathcal V^L\), denoiser \(f_\phi\), noise module \(\sigma(t)\), number of samples \(K\)
\State Initialize \(\texttt{accum}\gets 0_{L\times V}\)
\For{\(k=1\) \(\to\) \(K\)}
  \State sample \(t_k \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)\)
  \State compute total noise \(\;\sigma\gets \sigma(t_k)\)
  \State set mask‐probability \(\beta \gets 1 - e^{-\sigma}\)
  \State corrupt \(\tilde x\) by masking each token of \(x\) independently with probability \(\beta\)
  \State compute logits \(\;z^{(k)} = f_\phi(\tilde x)\in\mathbb R^{L\times V}\)
  \State convert to probabilities \(\;p^{(k)}\gets\mathrm{softmax}(z^{(k)})\)
  \State \(\texttt{accum}\;+\!=\;p^{(k)}\)
\EndFor
\State \Return \(\displaystyle\hat p = \tfrac1K\,\texttt{accum}\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Here \(\hat p_{i,v}\) is an unbiased Monte Carlo estimator of the true Bayesian posterior \(p(x_i=v\mid x)\).

\subsection{Derived Outputs}

From \(\hat p\in\Delta^{L\times V}\) we obtain:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Maximum‐a‐posteriori decode:} \(\hat x_i=\arg\max_v \hat p_{i,v}.\)
  \item \textbf{Perplexity:}
    \[
      \mathrm{PPL} = \exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac1L
      \sum_{i=1}^L
      \log \hat p_{i,x_i}\Bigr).
    \]
  \item \textbf{Predictive entropy:}
    \[
      H_i = -\sum_{v=1}^V \hat p_{i,v}\,\log\hat p_{i,v}.
    \]
  \item \textbf{Marginal variance:}
    \[
      V_i = \sum_{v=1}^V \hat p_{i,v}\,(1-\hat p_{i,v}).
    \]
  \item \textbf{Mutual information (epistemic uncertainty):}
    \[
      I_i = H_i \;-\; \frac1K\sum_{k=1}^K H\bigl(p^{(k)}_i\bigr),
    \]
    where \(p^{(k)}_i\) is the softmax at position \(i\) on the \(k\)-th corruption.
\end{itemize}

All of these require only elementary operations on \(\hat p\) (plus the \(K\) forward passes in Alg.~\ref{alg:marginalize}).

\subsection{Computational Complexity}

Let \(C\) be the cost of a single denoiser forward pass (comparable to evaluating GPT-2 Small once).  Mask sampling and softmax are \(O(LV)\), negligible versus the \(O(C)\) of a transformer.

\[
\boxed{
\mathrm{Cost}_{\mathrm{marginal}} = K\,C \;+\; O(KLV)\approx K\,C,
}
\]
where \(K\) is typically \(\le10\).  By contrast, autoregressive decoding of length \(L\) costs \(\approx L\,C\) sequentially (or \(\approx C\) for a single next-token batch) but cannot parallelize position‐wise uncertainty.

\subsection{Convergence and Finite‐Sample Error}

By the Strong Law of Large Numbers,
\[
\hat p \;\xrightarrow[K\to\infty]{a.s.}\; p(\cdot\mid x),
\]
and, under boundedness, Hoeffding’s inequality gives for each \(i,v\):
\[
\Pr\bigl(\bigl|\hat p_{i,v} - p_{i,v}\bigr| > \epsilon\bigr)
\;\le\;2\,e^{-2K\epsilon^2}.
\]
A union bound over \(i,v\) yields sup‐norm control and justifies the familiar \(O(1/\sqrt K)\) decay of the Monte Carlo error in metrics like PPL.

---

In the next section we empirically validate these predictions (PPL vs.\ \(K\), error‐entropy curves) and compare against GPT-2
\cite{radford2019language}.

\section{Experiments \& Results}

\subsection{Setup}
We load the pre‐trained RADD‐Tiny model from Ou et al. \cite{ou2025absorbing} via HuggingFace and evaluate on the WikiText-2 validation split.  We perform Monte Carlo marginal inference with $K\in\{1,4,8,16,32\}$ samples.  All perplexity and token‐accuracy results use the token‐wise estimator (Sec.~3.2); entropy/error curves use the full‐marginal estimator (Sec.~3.1).  As a baseline we compare to GPT-2 Small (124 M parameters) evaluated in parallel next‐token mode on the same data.

\subsection{Perplexity vs.\ Number of Samples}
Figure~\ref{fig:ppl_vs_k} shows MC‐marginal perplexity as a function of $1/\sqrt{K}$.  We observe:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $K=1$ (single draw) PPL: \(\mathrm{PPL}_1 = 8.23\).
  \item Asymptotic behavior $\mathrm{PPL}(K)\approx a + b/\sqrt{K}$ for $K\ge2$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{figs/ppl-vs-k.png}
  \caption{MC‐marginal PPL vs.\ $1/\sqrt{K}$.  The three‐term fit $a + b/\sqrt{K} + c\,(1/2)^K$ extrapolates to the asymptotic floor $a$.}
  \label{fig:ppl_vs_k}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection*{Jensen Slack \& K=1}
When fitting only $a + b/\sqrt{K}$ to $K\ge2$, the intercept $a$ underestimates the true asymptotic PPL by a constant ``Jensen slack’’ arising from the convexity of~$\exp(\cdot)$.  Moreover, the $K=1$ point corresponds to the single‐sample ELBO, which can exhibit unbounded slack (and thus lies off the $1/\sqrt K$ line).  By adding a small corrective term $c\,(1/2)^K$—reflecting the deterministic halving of the Jensen gap each time $K$ doubles—we obtain an excellent fit ($R^2=0.971$) and accurate extrapolation to $K\to\infty$.

