\section{Introduction}
Many question answering systems use knowledge representation schemes like knowledge graphs to enable the structured organization of semantically relevant information in a way that machines can process and reason over effectively \cite{thomas2022knowledge,martinez2023survey}. Graph-structured knowledge representation is particularly advantageous for answering complex or context-sensitive queries, as it supports semantic understanding, hierarchical reasoning, and cross-referencing of concepts related to questions posed in natural language. In the legal domain, researchers have developed graph-based methods for representing and analyzing legal documents \cite{boer2008metalex,athan2015legalruleml,filtz2021knowledge}. These representations allow systems to capture not only the hierarchical and interdependent nature of statutes, ordinances, and regulations, but also the logical conditions, exceptions, and temporal validity embedded in legal provisions, and have been used for legal question answering \cite{martinez2023survey}.

Obligation Logic Graph (OLG) \cite{servantez2023computable} is a recent graph-structured knowledge representation scheme designed to capture the deontic content of legal documents. Originally developed for ``computational contracts'', the OLG is designed to represent the elements of deontic logic \cite{jones1992deontic} covering obligations, prohibitions, and permissions, and hence is a prime candidate for generalization to legal documents that are not contracts. The OLG paper extracts a ``faithful representation'' of the deontic content from the text of the document, to construct the \textit{obligation graph} from the ``key entities, relationships, and formulas needed to create a computational representation of the business logic in a clause''. We would like to explore how this graph structure, if duly extended, can support legal question answering.

The goal of this short, work-in-progress paper is twofold: 1) we evaluate the expressivity of OLG for legal documents such as municipal ordinances and state laws (Sec. \ref{sec:OLG}); after identifying some expressivity gaps, we extend the OLG model by adding more constructs that capture more legal semantics (Sec. \ref{sec:OLG++}); and 2) we show that the extended OLG model can answer questions that are difficult with comparable methods (Sec. \ref{sec:QuestionAnswering}). We recognize that using NLP techniques to extract the OLG++ model from text is a significant and challenging task. But we leave it outside the scope of this paper.

