The example illustrates both advantages and semantic shortcomings of the current OLG model. The constructs of the model capture the essence of the legal rule. However, for this example, we make the following observations.  
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item While temporal concepts and relationships are represented, locational entities and relationships are not, making it hard to represent, for instance, zoning laws.
    \item Logical dependencies are captured, but logical groupings are not expressible. A rule like ``A food truck may operate in residential zones only if:
(a) The operation occurs during daylight hours (6AM-6PM) AND the location is at least 500 feet from any school; OR
(b) The operation is part of a permitted neighborhood event AND has received written approval from 60\% of residents.'' cannot be suitably represented.
    \item The specification of \textit{what} node or the object of a \texttt{what} relationship (we call it \textit{whatRel}) is underconstrained. Node \textit{n8} in Table \ref{tab:nodes} represents multiple semantic entities (e.g., street) that belong to a larger class(e.g., public location). Indeed, complex expressions like this are human interpretable but do not have sufficient \textit{semantic granularity}. Secondly, a simple question like ``what restrictions apply to businesses on sidewalks'' would require the question answering system to perform an additional inquiry.
\end{enumerate}
We notice that the OLG model lacks information specificity and expressivity to apply to city/state/federal laws, prompting us to refine and extend the model while adding constraints on node contents. We call this the OLG++ model.
