{Locational} & spatial & proximity & Within city limits \\ \cline{2-4}
& proximity\_to & distance & Within 500ft of school \\
\hline
Membership & member\_of & type & see text in Section \ref{sec:OLG++}\\ \hline
%Context & contextual & description, validity & During normal operations \\ \hline
%Formula & formula\_rel & operation & Temperature calculation \\ \hline
Precedence & precedence & level & Priority over other rules \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}
We assume that all node instances of the OLG and OLG++ models share a common set of base properties \texttt{id}, \texttt{created\_date}, \newline \texttt{modified\_date}, and \texttt{status} to keep track of rule changes. 


\noindent\textbf{Party and PartyGroup.} Consider a legal rule for community
kitchens whereby a food truck operator AND the community kitchen manager MUST jointly: 1. Sign and maintain the food safety inspection log and 2. Verify proper temperature control during food transfers. We use the party group node to capture the jointness of the obligation. Each party group node is connected to a set of Party nodes (with atomic entities) via a has\_member relationship. Consider a rule on the community kitchen that states (a) A food truck operator MUST store their vehicle overnight at a licensed community kitchen and (b) the commissary kitchen operator MUST maintain a daily log of all food trucks stored. In this case, there are two separate obligations that the food truck operator and the kitchen operator must fulfill, which are represented by two distinct paths in the OLG++ graph. A special case of party groups involves \textit{collectives} where individual parties are included by reference (employees of an organization). In this case, the is\_collective property of the party group node is set to true. In this case, this collective group forms a ``legal person'' and all related obligations are associated with this legal person. When the party group is defined with respect to a semantic entity (e.g., members of the city council) the party group node is linked to the city council node (of type SemanticEntity) via the \textit{member\_of} edge. In the case, where a collective party group has different subgroups with different obligations for each subgroup, we will create multiple collectives, that are connected to their own obligations.

\noindent \textbf{SemanticEntity.} Instances of Party from the OLG model are semantic entities. They are typically noun phrases which can be classified as a known semantic category via Named Entity Recognition or mapped to an external ontology. In OLG++, an obligation (or event) trigger may often refer to a semantic entity that is not a party. For example, a mobile kitchen facility must store raw chicken in a freezer below below 40$^\circ$F, and the thermometer used for verification must be calibrated monthly. Here, ``raw chicken'' and ``thermometer'' are semantic entities. Two semantic entities may be related via a \textit{subclass\_of} relationship or any other \textit{application-dependent relationships} such as \textit{stored\_at} that links the ``raw chicken'' node to the ``temperature''. The OLG++ models allows Party nodes to use \textit{subclass\_of} as well as other semantic relationships. We recognize that selecting appropriate reusable ontologies and foundational knowledge graphs for mapping semantic entities is a larger research question, but we leave it outside the scope of this short paper.

\noindent \textbf{Location.} Location referring to specific places (like a street address), named (e.g., San Diego Airport) or unnamed regions (e.g., 300 meters from a school) are designated semantic entities recognized in the OLG model. We include Location as a distinguished node type to accommodate questions of zoning laws and jusrisdictional questions. A location may have properties like boundaries which are a series of geographic conditions. Locations can also be classified via the \textit{subclass\_of} relationship. In the example in Sec. \ref{sec:OLG}, a sidewalk will be a subclass of a public location. In addition, topological (\textit{within, outside}), distance based (\textit{2 miles}) and directional (\textit{north of}) conditions are allowed. A composite condition like ``within 2 miles to the north of'' is the combination of three conditions using the logical operator ``AND''. 

\noindent \textbf{Jurisdiction.} Jurisdictions are spatial regions such that the obligations, prohibitions and permissions apply to this jurisdiction. It is a named or geographically specified spatial region that is related to a deontic trigger. Consider the following specification. ``The Central Business District (CBD) encompasses the area bounded by: \textit{Northern boundary: 12th Avenue, Eastern boundary: Park Street, Southern boundary: 4th Avenue, and Western boundary: Market Street}. All property owners in the CBD must provide street-level lighting between sunset and sunrise, and keep building facades in good repair.'' In OLG++, the location node corresponding to the CBD will be connected to the two obligations via \textit{has\_jurisdiction} edges. Two jurisdictions may be related and different obligations may apply to them. A follow-up rule may state ``Business owners of the Theatre District, bounded by 8th to 10th Avenue, between Broadway and Park Street, are allowed to have Marquee signs until midnight.'' In this case the Theater District is \textit{within} the CBD and the deontic permission applies to this district and not to the rest of the CBD. However, the inner district inherits the obligations on the CBD.

\noindent \textbf{LogicalGroup.} Logical group nodes are used to combine groups of logical operators. Using the example from Section \ref{sec:OLG} , ``A food truck may operate in residential zones only if: (a) The operation occurs during daylight hours (6AM-6PM) (condition node $c_1$) AND the location is at least 500 feet from any school ($c_2$); OR (b) The operation is part of a permitted neighborhood event ($c_3$) AND has received written approval from 60\% of residents ($c_4$).'' The conditions are represented as:
\begin{verbatim}
(m1:logicGroup {type:AND})-[logical:OR]
               -(m2:logicgroup {type:AND}),
(c1:condition)-[:MEMBER]->(m1), 
(c2:condition)-[:MEMBER]->(m1),
...
\end{verbatim}

\noindent \textbf{Exception Edges.} Two obligations $o_1, o_2$ may be related by the fact that $o_2$ is an exception to $o_1$. This restriction can be based on parties, jurisdiction, or any other semantic condition that defines the context of the exception. Consider a hypothetical law that limits all commercial buildings in a city to be at most 15 stories (obligation $o_1$), but within the business district, they can be at most 10 stories ($o_2$), and within the Theater district, at most 8 stories ($o_3$). Assuming the obligations have already been defined, the exception edges will be: \texttt{(o2)-[EXCEPTS]->(o1)} and \texttt{(o3)-[EXCEPTS]->(o2)}, creating an exception chain and defeasibility structure supported LegalRuleML language \cite{legalruleml-core-1.0} but using a simpler syntactic structure. Recall that the three locational jurisdictions are also linked via \textit{within} edges. The correspondence between the exception chain and the jurisdiction hierarchy allows us to create an explainable representation for the exception. Exception chains can also be specified with event triggers and conditions. ``If emergency response is activated, then emergency vehicles may park in any public parking space or on sidewalks.'' This passage has conditions: is emergency vehicle ($c_5$) and emergency response is activated ($c_6$). The OLG model creates an \textit{obligation\_trigger} node ($OT_1$) with description ``Permission: Parking in any public parking space or on sidewalks.'' This node will have a \textit{Logical Dependency} edge going to a node of type \textit{LogicNode} and subtype \textit{condition\_group} with \textit{evaluation} $c_5 \wedge c_6$. Node $OT_1$ will then have the edge \texttt{($OT_1$)-[EXCEPTS]->($p_1$)} where $p_1$ is a prohibition of parking on sidewalks. The explanatory structure for complex exceptions is defined by progressively restrictive logical conditions held by the \textit{LogicNode}. 


% ``Emergency vehicles may park in designated emergency zones. If emergency response is activated, then emergency vehicles may park in any public parking space or on sidewalks. If also an evacuation order is issued, then emergency vehicles may park in any location that does not block evacuation routes.'' 

\noindent\textbf{Overrides Edges.} The original OLG model does not explicitly model overriding of an obligation with another based on conditions. However, due to the needs of our application domain, we explicitly model rule defeasibility by creating an \textit{overrides} edge. In the food truck example, we may have the following rules regarding parking in residential zones:
\textit{Base Rule (ob1)}:
Food trucks are prohibited from parking within residential areas.
\textit{Exception Rule (ob2)}:
Food trucks with special permits may park in residential areas, overriding the base prohibition.
\textit{Emergency Rule (ob3)}:
During emergencies, food trucks must follow emergency access requirements, which override both the special permit allowances and the base prohibition.As in the case of exceptions, we can now add the edges:
\texttt{(ob2)-[OVERRIDES]->(ob1)} and \texttt{(ob3)-[OVERRIDES]->(ob2)}. We note that overrides are distinct from exceptions. An exception models an construct like Rule1 \textit{unless} Condition1 in which case Rule2. An override models the situation where both Rule1 and Rule2 might apply, but Rule2 takes priority. Hence an override models a priority hierarchy \cite{prakken2015law}. Together, these constructs serve as the building blocks that can be used to create more complex deontic structure.



%With these extensions, OLG++ provides a richer framework for capturing and reasoning about legal provisions, making it suitable for applications including legislative analysis. 
%We show next how this model can be used for legal question-answering.

\section{Question Answering using OLG++}
\label{sec:QuestionAnswering}
We illustrate a couple of examples of how this model might be used to answer complex questions. We assume we have a Neo4J graph database that stores regulations following the OLG++ model. The following queries are written in Cypher, Neo4J's query language \cite{francis2018cypher}.

\noindent \textbf{Q1.} ``What are the valid parking locations for food trucks in San Diego during lunch hours?''

\noindent \textbf{Step 1. Find food truck related parties}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (p:Party {type: 'party_group', 
               group_type: 'food_truck'})
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 2. Match San Diego jurisdiction and locations within it}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (j:Location {type: 'jurisdiction', 
                   name: 'San Diego'})
MATCH (l:Location {type: 'specific_location'})
MATCH (l)-[:location_predicate {type: 'within'}]->(j)
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 3. Match lunch hours temporal constraint}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (t:Timex)
WHERE t.description CONTAINS 'lunch hours'
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 4. Match permissions for parking}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (et:event_trigger {type: 'parking'})
MATCH (what:What {type: 'parking_location'})
MATCH (ot:obligation_trigger)
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 5. Connect the components with proper edge types}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (et)-[:deontic_modality {type: 'permission'}]->(ot)
MATCH (ot)-[:whatRel]->(what)
MATCH (what)-[:spatial]->(l)
MATCH (ot)-[:temporal {type: 'during'}]->(t)
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 6. Check for location restrictions}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (lr:LogicNode {type: 'condition_group'})
WHERE NOT EXISTS {
    MATCH (lr)-[:proximity_to]->(restricted:Location)
    WHERE restricted.type IN ['school', 'hospital']
    AND lr.evaluation = 'prohibited'}
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 7. Check for overrides and exceptions}
\begin{verbatim}
WHERE NOT EXISTS {
    MATCH (override:LogicNode)-[:override]->(ot)
    WHERE override.evaluation = 'prohibited'}
AND NOT EXISTS {
    MATCH (exc:Defeasibility)-[:exception]->(ot)
    WHERE exc.description CONTAINS 'emergency'}
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 8. Return valid parking locations with their constraints}
\begin{verbatim}
RETURN DISTINCT l.description as Location, 
l.lat as Latitude, l.long as Longitude, 
t.description as TimeFrame, 
what.description as ParkingRules ORDER BY Location;
\end{verbatim}

\noindent \textbf{Q2.} ``If our food truck prepares food in Tijuana but serves in San Diego, which safety regulations apply?''

\noindent \textbf{Step 1. Match jurisdictions}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (tj:Location {type: 'jurisdiction', 
                    name: 'Tijuana'})
MATCH (sd:Location {type: 'jurisdiction', 
                    name: 'San Diego'})
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 2. Match food preparation and service locations}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (prep:Location {type: 'specific_location'})-
            [:location_predicate {type: 'within'}]->(tj)
MATCH (serv:Location {type: 'specific_location'})-
            [:location_predicate {type: 'within'}]->(sd)
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 3. Match relevant obligations and regulations}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (ft:Party {type: 'party_group', 
                 group_type: 'food_truck'})
MATCH (ot:obligation_trigger), (ref:Reference)
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 4. Food safety obligations}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (safety_obl:obligation_trigger)-[:whatRel]->(what:What)
WHERE what.description CONTAINS 'food safety'
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 5. Match inspection requirements}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (insp:obligation_trigger)-[:whatRel]->
                                (insp_what:What)
WHERE insp_what.description CONTAINS 'inspection'
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 6. Connect with health code references}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (safety_obl)-[:subject_to]->(ref)
WHERE ref.description CONTAINS 'Health Code'
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 7. Match jurisdiction-specific requirements}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (safety_obl)-[:deontic_modality 
                {type: 'obligation'}]->(ft)
MATCH (insp)-[:deontic_modality 
                {type: 'obligation'}]->(ft)
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 8. Check for cross-border specific rules}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (cb_rules:LogicNode {type: 'condition_group'})
WHERE cb_rules.description CONTAINS 'cross-border'
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 9. Check for prerequisite requirements}
\begin{verbatim}
MATCH (prereq:Edge:prerequisite)-[:prerequisite]
                                    ->(safety_obl)
\end{verbatim}
\textbf{Step 10. Return obligations by jurisdiction}
\begin{verbatim}
RETURN CASE WHEN safety_obl-[:spatial]->(:Location)-
                    [:location_predicate]->(tj) 
        THEN 'Tijuana' 
        ELSE 'San Diego' 
    END as Jurisdiction, ref.clause as RegulatoryCode, 
    what.description as SafetyRequirement, 
    insp_what.description as InspectionRequirement, 
    prereq.description as Prerequisites, 
    cb_rules.description as CrossBorderRules
ORDER BY Jurisdiction;
\end{verbatim}


\section{Conclusion}
We presented a work-in-progress report on our effort to develop a semantic extension of the Obligation Logic Graph to represent legal knowledge. We have also demonstrated that the model is capable of expressing complex legal questions as Cypher queries. Our ongoing work includes the development of a formal analysis of the expressive power of the language, and developing an LLM-based algorithm to faithfully extract the OLG++ graph from a corpus.


