    % Clearly, $S$ is a dominating set of $H$.
    % Suppose that there is a vertex $v \in V(H) \setminus S$ such that $S' \coloneqq (S \setminus \{u, w\}) \cup \{v\}$ is a dominating set of $H$ for some $u, w \in S$.
    % Since $S'$ is not a vertex cover of $G$, there is an edge $e$ that is not covered by $S'$.
    % This implies that $x_e$ and $y_e$ are not dominated by $S'$, which gives a contradiction.
    % 
    % Conversely, suppose that $S$ is a 2-minimal dominating set of $H$.
    % If $S$ contains both $x_e$ and $y_e$ for some edge $e = \{u, v\}$, $(S \setminus \{x_e, y_e\}) \cup \{u\}$ is a dominating set of $H$, contradicting the 2-minimality of $S$.
    % Moreover, if $S$ contains exactly one of $x_e$ and $y_e$, say $x_e$, then $S$ must contains at least one of $u$ and $v$ as otherwise $y_e$ is not dominated.
    % This implies that $S \setminus \{x_e\}$ is still a dominating set of $H$, which contradicts the minimality of $S$.
