\subsection{Impact of Random Access Intensity}
We next evaluate the sensitivity of inference throughput to the fraction of random memory accesses under a high raw BER of $10^{-3}$. Fig.~\ref{fig:rand_intensity} shows token throughput as the random-access ratio increases from 0\% to 10\% for six RS codeword lengths. Under purely sequential traffic (0\% random), throughput increases consistently with codeword size, rising from 13.90 tokens/s (64B) to 18.05 tokens/s (2048B). This trend highlights the benefit of longer codewords in amortizing ECC overhead: larger codewords have higher code rates, reducing redundancy and improving effective memory bandwidth.

However, introducing even a small fraction of random access disrupts this advantage. At 2\% random access, throughput drops across all cases, with larger codewords most affected. The 64B case decreases slightly to 13.85 tokens/s (–0.4\%), while the 2048B case drops more sharply to 14.26 tokens/s (–21.0\%). At 10\% random access, 64B still maintains 13.64 tokens/s (–1.9\%), while 256B and 2048B fall to 11.87 (–19.2\%) and 7.31 tokens/s (–59.5\%), respectively. This pattern reflects the impact of access amplification. When a CRC failure occurs on a 34B chunk, it escalates into a full-codeword read or read-modify-write, and the associated overhead scales with codeword size. Consequently, in workloads with even modest random activity, moderate codeword lengths (256B–512B) offer the best trade-off between ECC efficiency and access amplification, while very large codewords become increasingly inefficient.

