\subsection{Importance-Adaptive Protection}
Fig.~\ref{fig:bitfield_sensitivity} presents the impact of targeted bit flips in different BF16 fields on model accuracy for the LLaMA 3.1 8B model. We randomly inject faults into the sign, exponent, and mantissa fields by flipping bits at rates ranging from $10^{-8}$ to $10^{-3}$. The model is evaluated on two benchmarks: PIQA~\cite{bisk2020piqa}, which assesses physical reasoning, and MMLU~\cite{hendrycks2020measuring}. The results reveal that sign and mantissa corruption has minimal effect, even at the highest error rate. Accuracy remains above 97\% of the error-free baseline, demonstrating high resilience to noise in these bit-planes. In contrast, exponent errors result in severe degradation: at a flip rate of $10^{-3}$, PIQA accuracy drops to 61.7\% and MMLU to 38.1\%. These findings indicate that the exponent bits are far more critical for preserving numerical integrity, and thus should be prioritized in error protection schemes.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figure/normalized_acc.pdf}
  \caption{Normalized LLaMA 3.1 8B accuracy under bit flips in BF16 sign, exponent, and mantissa fields on (a) PIQA and (b) MMLU.}
  \label{fig:bitfield_sensitivity}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:eff_bw_exp} explores the efficiency gains of concentrating ECC protection solely on the exponent field. We compare effective HBM bandwidth utilization between exponent-only and full-bit ECC strategies across RS codeword lengths ranging from 64B to 2048B, under three increasingly aggressive BERs ($10^{-5}$, $10^{-4}$, and $10^{-3}$). At $10^{-5}$, exponent-only ECC achieves 98.43\% utilization at 64B and 98.49\% at 2048B, while full-bit ECC reaches only 93.91\% and 94.73\%, respectively. As the BER rises to $10^{-4}$, the efficiency gap widens further. For instance, with 256B codewords, exponent-only ECC maintains 98.23\% bandwidth, compared to 90.39\% for full-bit ECC. Even under extreme noise at $10^{-3}$, exponent-only protection holds up: 93.48\% at 64B and 95.69\% at 2048B, whereas full-bit ECC drops to 80.90\% and 89.00\%, respectively. Across all settings, adaptive protection consistently reduces ECC-related traffic overhead, with bandwidth improvements reaching up to 12.6\%.

Together, these results demonstrate the effectiveness of importance-aware ECC in balancing robustness and efficiency. By aligning protection strength with data sensitivity, focusing on the exponent field, this approach  reduces bandwidth consumption and decoder workload, while preserving accuracy under high error rates. Importance-adaptive ECC thus offers a compelling direction for lowering HBM cost and controller complexity, opening the door to more scalable and resilient AI memory systems.

