\begin{abstract}
Automotive safety and security are paramount in the rapidly advancing landscape of vehicular technology. Building safe and secure vehicles demands a profound understanding of automotive systems, particularly in safety and security. Traditional learning approaches, such as reading materials or observing demonstrations, often fail to provide the practical, hands-on experience essential for developing this expertise. For novice users, gaining access to automotive-grade systems and mastering their associated hardware and software can be challenging and overwhelming. In this paper, we present a novel, affordable, and flexible exploration platform, \hema, that enables users to gain practical, hands-on insights into the security compromises of micro-electromechanical systems (MEMS) sensors, a critical component in modern ADAS systems. Furthermore, we discuss the unique challenges and design considerations involved in creating such a platform, emphasizing its role in enhancing the understanding of automotive safety and security. This framework serves as an invaluable resource for educators, researchers, and practitioners striving to build expertise in the field.


%Automotive safety and security are paramount in the rapidly advancing landscape of vehicular technology. Building safe and secure vehicles requires a deep understanding of automotive systems, particularly in safety and security domains. However, traditional approaches, such as reading materials or observing expert demonstrations, often fail to provide the practical, hands-on learning required to develop this expertise. Novices and industry professionals alike need interactive platforms to explore and address real-world challenges. To bridge this gap, we present HELP (Hands-on Exploration and Learning Platforms), a standardized architecture designed to serve as a foundation for developing future exploration platforms. HELP offers a modular, scalable, and adaptable framework that enables experiential learning, equipping users to analyze, understand, and tackle automotive safety and security challenges. Inspired by HELP, platforms such as HAWA, AutoHAL, and VECAEP have already demonstrated its versatility and impact. Additionally, HELP enables researchers to design and develop novel exploration platforms by adopting and extending this architecture to address emerging challenges. This paper outlines the HELP architecture, highlights its applications in existing platforms, and demonstrates its potential to revolutionize vehicular technology education, empowering researchers, educators, and industry professionals to drive innovation in safety and security.

\end{abstract}

